        heading: 'The Scaffold Has Gone Digital',
        body:
          'The first thing Hawthorne shows the reader is a crowd assembled to watch a punishment. The scaffold has no function except visibility: the point is not that Hester suffers but that everyone sees her suffer, and that she stands there long enough for the image to fix itself in the town’s memory.\n\nIn 2026 that machinery is ambient. A phone recording, a screenshot of a decade-old post, a name attached to a bad twenty seconds, and the crowd assembles in hours without anyone needing to be summoned to a market square. The permanence Hawthorne could only achieve by sewing the letter onto her dress is now supplied by search results, which is why the novel’s central problem, how a person is supposed to live afterward, has become an ordinary one.\n\nHawthorne offers no clean answer, but he does insist on the difference between punishment and moral bookkeeping. The town gets Hester wrong for years, revises its verdict slowly, and never quite admits it was mistaken. Reading her story now is a way of asking whether our own systems of exposure leave any route back, or whether we have rebuilt the scaffold with no term of sentence attached to it.',
