          'Two people commit the same act. One is displayed on a platform with an infant in her arms, and the other goes on preaching to the congregation that condemned her. Hawthorne never lets the reader forget the asymmetry, and it is not only a matter of Dimmesdale’s cowardice. Hester is visible because pregnancy made her visible, and because a woman’s conduct was the community’s business in a way a minister’s inner life was not.\n\nThe uneven distribution of consequence remains a live subject. Public scandals in 2026 still tend to attach more durably to women, whose names stay searchable while men in the same story quietly recover their standing, and arguments about reputation, privacy, and slut-shaming replay the scaffold scene with newer equipment. Hester’s refusal to name her partner also raises a question we are still arguing about: how much of a private life the public is owed.\n\nWhat keeps Hester modern is that she never spends the novel apologizing. She takes the sentence, withholds the explanation, does the work, raises her daughter, and eventually comes back to wear the letter because she has decided that it is hers. That is closer to reclaiming a story than to enduring one, and it is why she reads less like a victim than like the only free adult in Boston.',
