          'Twain described the book as one in which a sound heart and a deformed conscience come into collision and the conscience suffers defeat. Huck has absorbed everything his society taught him: that Jim is property, that helping him escape is theft, and that a boy who does such a thing will go to hell for it. He never questions any of that theology, not once.\n\nThat is what makes the crisis at the Phelps farm so powerful. Huck does the thing he has been taught is right, writes the letter, and feels clean for the first time in days; then he starts remembering Jim on the raft and cannot make the feeling hold. When he tears the paper up he believes he is choosing damnation, and Twain lets him keep believing it, because a Huck who understood that he was being noble would be a far smaller character.\n\nThe irony runs entirely in the reader’s favor and never in Huck’s. Every time he calls himself wicked we understand that his instincts are sounder than the whole moral apparatus of the antebellum South, and the joke turns back on the reader’s own certainties. Twain built one of the great moral scenes in American literature out of a boy condemning himself for an act of ordinary decency.',
